---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const lastUpdated = "September 18, 2026";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Lift Recorder Privacy Policy - bneuhausz.dev"
  description="Privacy policy for the Lift Recorder Android app."
  canonical="https://bneuhausz.dev/apps/lift-recorder/privacy/"
>
  <section
    class="prose dark:prose-invert text-left max-w-screen-md mx-auto px-8 pb-16"
  >
    <p class="text-sm text-amber-200/70">
      <a href="/apps/lift-recorder/">← Back to Lift Recorder</a>
    </p>

    <h1 class="text-3xl">Lift Recorder — Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-amber-200/70">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

    <p>
      <strong>Lift Recorder</strong> has no account, no server, and no analytics or
      crash reporting of its own. Your videos, any sound you choose to record, and
      the workout tags and personal records you enter are stored on your own device
      and are never uploaded by the app. The only third party involved is Google,
      through the ads and the in-app purchase described below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      The app is published by Bálint Neuhausz (Hungary), the data controller
      under the GDPR. Contact: <a href="mailto:contact@bneuhausz.dev"
        >contact@bneuhausz.dev</a
      >. Full business details are in the <a href="/imprint/">Imprint</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Your videos</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The app uses the <strong>camera</strong> only to record your sets, and saves
        each recording to your device's gallery, in the album you choose.
      </li>
      <li>
        To show your clips in the app, it reads the videos in the albums it has
        recorded into, after Android asks for your permission to access videos.
        It lists only those clips.
      </li>
      <li>
        Videos <strong>never leave your device</strong> through the app — no upload,
        no cloud. A clip only goes somewhere else when you share it yourself through
        Android's share sheet, and then only to the app you pick.
      </li>
      <li>
        There is no account and no login, so the app does not know who you are.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Sound (version 1.1 and later; off by default)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Recordings are <strong>silent by default</strong>. With sound off, the app
        never opens the microphone, even if you granted the permission earlier.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you turn on <em>Record sound</em> (in Settings, or with the microphone
        button on the camera), the app first explains what that means and then asks
        Android for microphone access. The microphone is used only while a
        recording is running.
      </li>
      <li>
        The sound is saved <strong>only as the audio track of that clip</strong>, on
        your device. The app never uploads or analyses it; if you share a clip, its
        sound goes with it.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can turn sound off at any time, and remove the microphone permission in
        your device's settings. Without the permission, the app switches sound off
        and keeps recording without it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Workout tags and personal records (version 1.1 and later)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If you tag a set, the app stores what you entered — the exercise, the weight
        lifted, reps and RPE — together with the clip's file name and recording
        date. Exercise names you add, and personal records you type in by hand
        (with an optional date), are stored the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        This is used only to label and filter your clips and to work out your
        <strong>personal records</strong>, which happens on your device. Apart from
        these workout entries, the app records no body measurements, heart rate or
        other health data, and it does not connect to Health Connect or any other
        service.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is kept in the app's private storage on your device and is
        <strong>never uploaded by the app, sent to us, or shared</strong> with
        anyone, including advertisers (apart from your own Android backup, below).
        It is not included when you share a clip.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can change or clear a tag and remove records in the app. Deleting a
        clip in the app deletes its tag too — unless that set is one of your
        personal records, which is kept and marked "video deleted" so it stays on
        your records list. If you delete a clip outside the app, its tag is removed
        the first time you open your clips at least 30 days later with access to
        all your videos. Clearing the app's storage or uninstalling it deletes all
        of this.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Android backup</h2>
    <p>
      Like most Android apps, Lift Recorder lets Android include its private
      data — your tags, personal records and settings — in your device's own
      backup and when you move to a new phone. That backup is made by Android to
      your Google Account under Google's terms and is never sent to us; on
      Android 9 and newer with a screen lock it is end-to-end encrypted, and we
      cannot read it. You can turn backup off in your device's settings. Your
      videos are not part of this app backup.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">How your data is protected</h2>
    <p>
      Everything the app keeps is in Android's app-private storage, which other
      apps cannot read, and the app itself sends none of it anywhere. Your clips
      live in your gallery under Android's normal media permissions. Google's
      advertising SDK sends its data over encrypted (TLS) connections, and Google
      keeps it according to its own retention policy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Advertising (Google AdMob)</h2>
    <p>
      In the free version, ads are served by <strong>Google AdMob</strong>. To
      show and measure ads and to prevent fraud, Google's advertising SDK may
      collect device and usage information, including your device's <strong
        >Advertising ID</strong
      > and other device identifiers, approximate location derived from your IP
      address, how you interact with the app and its ads (such as app launches
      and taps), and diagnostic and crash information about the app's and the
      SDK's performance. The app gives it none of your videos, sound, tags or
      records. Google processes this as an independent controller under its own
      terms — see
      <a
        href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Privacy Policy</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the European Economic Area, the UK, and Switzerland, the app shows a
      Google-certified
      <strong>consent prompt</strong> before personalized ads are used, so you can
      accept or decline. You can change your choice any time from <em
        >Settings → Ad privacy options</em
      >
      in the app.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Removing ads</h2>
    <p>
      The one-time <strong>"Remove ads"</strong> purchase is handled by
      <strong>Google Play Billing</strong> — the app never sees your card or payment
      details. Each time it starts, the app asks Google Play whether you own the
      purchase, so it can restore it on a new phone. Once ads are removed, the app
      no longer initialises the advertising SDK or requests ads. (Right after a
      reinstall, an ad may still load until Google Play has confirmed your
      purchase.)
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Children</h2>
    <p>
      Lift Recorder is a general-audience app and is <strong
        >not directed at children</strong
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account and nothing stored on a server of ours, so there is
      nothing for us to access or delete — everything the app keeps is on your
      device. In the app you can clear tags, remove records, delete clips and turn
      sound off, and in your device's settings you can remove the camera,
      microphone or video permissions. Clearing the app's storage or uninstalling
      it deletes its data from your device (a copy in your own Android backup
      follows your backup settings), and your saved videos stay in your gallery
      until you delete them. For the ad-related data Google collects, use the
      Google links above and your device's privacy controls. You can contact us at <a
        href="mailto:contact@bneuhausz.dev">contact@bneuhausz.dev</a
      >, and under the GDPR you may lodge a complaint with your local data
      protection authority.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl">Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, this page and the "last updated" date above will
      be updated.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
